ast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ°Blessed are ye, when men shall hate you, and when they shall separate you from their company, and shall reproach you, and cast out your name as evil, for the Son of man's sake.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’Rejoice ye in that day, and leap for joy: for, behold, your reward is great in heaven: for in the like manner did their fathers unto the prophets.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFBut woe unto you that are rich! for ye have received your consolation.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjWoe unto you that are full! for ye shall hunger. Woe unto you that laugh now! for ye shall mourn and weep.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcWoe unto you, when all men shall speak well of you! for so did their fathers to the false prophets.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQBut I say unto you which hear, Love your enemies, do good to them which hate you,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHbless them that curse you, and pray for them which despitefully use you.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠAnd unto him that smiteth thee on the one cheek offer also the other; and him that taketh away thy cloke forbid not to take thy coat also.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`Give to every man that asketh of thee; and of him that taketh away thy goods ask them not again.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGAnd as ye would that men should do to you, do ye also to them likewise.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse$¦verses•„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcFor if ye love them which love you, what thank have ye? for sinners also love those that love them.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd if ye do good to them which do good to you, what thank have ye? for sinners also do even the same.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚And if ye lend to them of whom ye hope to receive, what thank have ye? for sinners also lend to sinners, to receive as much again.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙËBut love ye your enemies, and do good, and lend, hoping for nothing again; and your reward shall be great, and ye shall be the children of the Highest: for he is kind unto the unthankful and to the evil.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.¦layout ªlast_verse$…¤book)§chapter«first_verse%ªlast_verse&¦verses’„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuJudge not, and ye shall not be judged: condemn not, and ye shall not be condemned: forgive, and ye shall be forgiven:¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÙgive, and it shall be given unto you; good measure, pressed down, and shaken together, and running over, shall men give into your bosom. For with the same measure that ye mete withal it shall be measured to you again.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book)§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse*¦verses”„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhAnd he spake a parable unto them, Can the blind lead the blind? shall they not both fall into the ditch?¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[The disciple is not above his master: but every one that is perfect shall be as his master.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsAnd why beholdest thou the mote that is in thy brother's eye, but perceivest not the beam that is in thine own eye?¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚ5Either how canst thou say to thy brother, Brother, let me pull out the mote that is in thine eye, when thou thyself beholdest not the beam that is in thine own eye? Thou hypocrite, cast out first the beam out of thine own eye, and then shalt thou see clearly to pull out the mote that is in thy brother's eye.¦layout ªlast_verse*…¤book)§chapter«first_verse+ªlast_verse-¦verses“„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeFor a good tree bringeth not forth corrupt fruit; neither doth a corrupt tree bring forth good fruit.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyFor every tree is known by his own fruit. For of thorns men do not gather figs, nor of a bramble bush gather they grapes.¦layout ªlast_verse,„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙâA good man out of the good treasure of his heart bringeth forth that which is good; and an evil man out of the evil treasure of his heart bringeth forth that which is evil: for of the abundance of the heart his mouth speaketh.¦layout ªlast_verse-…¤book)§chapter«first_verse.ªlast_verse1¦verses”„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBAnd why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?¦layout ªlast_verse.„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcWhosoever cometh to me, and heareth my sayings, and doeth them, I will shew you to whom he is like:¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙØhe is like a man which built an house, and digged deep, and laid the foundation on a rock: and when the flood arose, the stream beat vehemently upon that house, and could not shake it: for it was founded upon a rock.¦layout ªlast_verse0„¥verse1¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ×But he that heareth, and doeth not, is like a man that without a foundation built an house upon the earth; against which the stream did beat vehemently, and immediately it fell; and the ruin of that house was great.¦layout ªlast_verse1